                "<b>Article 3: Rule of Law and Truth (Asha)</b>",
                "The law is the manifestation of the collective will and wisdom of the nation. All laws, regulations, and judicial verdicts must align with the principles of truth, science-based justice, the International Bill of Human Rights, and environmental sustainability (Asha). No source or expediency stands above the law.",
                "",
                "Chapter II: Status, Boundaries, and Symbolic Duties of the Constitutional Monarchy (Based on the Swedish Model)",
                "<b>Article 4: Symbolic Nature and Total Separation of the Monarch from Power</b>",
                "Following the constitutional framework of Sweden (The Instrument of Government 1974), the Monarchy is a purely symbolic institution, completely devoid of any political, legislative, executive, or judicial power. The Monarch is the symbol of national unity, historical continuity, and harmony among all ethnicities and segments of Iran (Guardian of Armaiti) and is strictly barred from intervening in sovereign affairs.",
                "<b>Article 5: Removal of Executive Powers and the Nomination of the Prime Minister</b>",
                " In accordance with advanced Scandinavian (Swedish) laws, the Monarch plays no role in appointing military commanders or nominating the Prime Minister. The duty of nominating the Prime Minister and tasking them with forming the government rests entirely with the Speaker of the Parliament (the Speaker of the House of Representatives), who is elected by the people. The signing of laws by the Monarch is a purely ceremonial act, and the Monarch has no right to veto any legislation.",
                "<b>Article 6: Mechanism for Dethronement or Abdication</b>",
                "Should the Monarch violate the boundaries of the Constitution or breach their oath, as determined by the Supreme Court or a two-thirds majority of the Parliament, they shall be automatically dethroned. The Parliament shall then decide on the continuity of the institution, a change of person, or a referendum regarding the structure of the office.",
                "",
                "Chapter III: Fundamental Rights, Liberties, and Citizen Safeguards",
                "<b>Article 7: Comprehensive Equality of Gender, Ethnicity, and Belief</b>",
                " All citizens of Iran, regardless of gender, descent, language, ethnicity, race, philosophical, religious, or political views, or personal orientations, are completely equal before the law. The application of constructive affirmative action in favor of historically marginalized groups to establish true justice is lawful.",
                "<b>Article 8: Inviolability of Conscience (Daena) and the Private Sphere</b>",
                "Conscience, belief, and personal religion are strictly individual and free domains. Inquisition of beliefs and the establishment of an official state religion are prohibited in the Constitution. The residence, correspondence, digital communications, and privacy of citizens are immune from any arbitrary intrusion, wiretapping, or inspection without a clear and substantiated judicial order.",
                "<b>Article 9: Freedom of Expression, Media, and Access to Information</b>",
                "Freedom of thought, expression, press, art, and newspapers is guaranteed. Prior censorship is forbidden. Any monopoly over broadcasting media by the government or the monarchy is illegitimate. All citizens have the right to free and unfiltered access to the flow of information and the global internet.",
                "<b>Article 10: Judicial Security, Prohibition of Torture, and the Presumption of Innocence</b>",
                "Every accused person is presumed innocent until their guilt is proven in a public court, before a jury, and with the right to an independent counsel. Any physical or psychological abuse (torture), coerced confession, prolonged solitary confinement, and degrading treatment are strictly prohibited, and violators shall be prosecuted without any statute of limitations.",
                "",
                "Chapter IV: Structure of Collective Wisdom (The Bicameral Legislative Branch)",
                "<b>Article 11: The House of Representatives (Parliament)</b>",
                " This house is composed of representatives elected through nationwide, free, secret, periodic, and proportional representation elections. The primary duties of this house include enacting ordinary legislation, approving the national budget, confirming the Prime Minister, and overseeing the executive branch.",
                "<b>Article 12: The Senate of Provinces and Experts</b>",
                "This house consists of equal representatives from the provinces and scientific/specialized elites of the country to defend local interests and sustainable development at the macro level. No law shall be finalized without being reviewed by both houses to prevent the centralization of power (Vohuman).",
                "",
                "Chapter V: The Executive Branch and the Servant Government (Khshathra)",
                "<b>Article 13: Formation of the Government and Accountability (UK Constitutional Model)</b>",
                " The head of government is the Prime Minister. The leader of the party or coalition that wins the majority of seats in the House of Representatives shall be tasked with forming the cabinet. The government is fully accountable to the Parliament, and the House of Representatives can dismiss the government or any minister at any time through a vote of no confidence.",
                "<b>Article 14: Control of the Executive Power and the Military</b>",
                " Military, border control, and security forces must remain entirely non-political and non-factional, acting under the direct oversight and command of the civilian government and the Parliament. The intervention of the armed forces in economic, commercial, political, and electoral affairs is strictly prohibited and considered a criminal offense.",
                "",
                "Chapter VI: Independent Judiciary and the Arbiter of Truth (The Court of Asha)","<b></b>",
                "<b>Article 15: Absolute Independence of the Judiciary</b>",
                "The Judiciary is an independent institution whose budget, management, and administration are run by the body of judges themselves and the Supreme Judicial Council, without any interference from the government or the monarch. Judges are subservient only to the law and their own conscience in issuing verdicts, and their tenure security is guaranteed.",
                "<b>Article 16: The Parliamentary Ombudsman (Based on the Swedish Model)</b>",
                "To prevent state officials from oppressing citizens, the House of Representatives shall appoint independent ombudsmen. This office has the right to investigate complaints from citizens against government agencies, the military, and municipalities, and can challenge their actions in court.",
                "<b>Article 17: The Supreme Constitutional Court</b>",
                "This court is the ultimate authority for interpreting the Constitution and assessing the conformity of laws passed by Parliament with human rights, democracy, and the decentralization of power. Any citizen who feels that a piece of legislation or an executive action has violated their fundamental rights has the right to file a direct complaint with this court.",
                "",
                "Chapter VII: Autonomous Administration of Provinces and Distribution of Wealth",
                "<b>Article 18: Vertical Division of Power (Provincial Assemblies)</b>",
                " Iran is divided into self-governing internal regions and provinces. Affairs related to education, local culture, urban and rural development, healthcare, local police, and regional budgets are completely devolved to 'Provincial Assemblies' and 'City and Village Councils' directly elected by the people of those regions. The capital is solely responsible for foreign policy, national defense, and the national currency.",
               "<b>Article 19: Equitable Distribution of National Wealth</b>",
               "Revenue generated from the country's natural resources is the collective property of the entire nation of Iran. Nonetheless, the budget allocation mechanism must be structured in a way that assigns a special share to the producing provinces and underdeveloped regions to ensure regional balance and justice.",
               "",
               "Chapter VIII: Sustainable Development and Environmental Protection (Haurvatat and Ameretat)",
